  "description": "Tells the story leading up to an OOM kill: MemAvailable decline, reclaim activity, page-cache drop, slab growth, then the kill. Watch this dashboard during a memory-heavy test to see the kernel's response unfold.",
  "editable": true,
  "fiscalYearStartMonth": 0,
  "graphTooltip": 0,
  "id": null,
  "links": [],
  "liveNow": true,
  "panels": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 6,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 100,
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "## Memory pressure \u2014 the story leading up to an OOM\n\nMemory failures rarely happen suddenly. There's almost always a several-minute curve where MemAvailable climbs down, the kernel starts reclaiming, swap activity rises, slab grows, then finally the OOM killer fires.\n\nThis dashboard tells that story. Each panel is a layer of the kernel's memory-management response. Watch them together to see the kill coming."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "row",
      "title": "The leading indicators (climb-down before the kill)",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 6
      },
      "id": 110,
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "MemAvailable fraction \u2014 the curve that ends in OOM",
      "description": "Fraction of memory still usable (accounting for reclaim). A steady decline toward 0 is the signal. Healthy = ~0.4-0.8.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 7
      },
      "id": 111,
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "node_memory_MemAvailable_bytes / node_memory_MemTotal_bytes",
          "legendFormat": "available fraction"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "percentunit",
          "custom": {
            "fillOpacity": 25,
            "lineWidth": 2,
            "stacking": {
              "mode": "none"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "OOM kills over time (the final event)",
      "description": "node_vmstat_oom_kill \u2014 every SIGKILL the kernel's OOM killer fired. Pair with the available-fraction panel to see cause\u2192effect.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 7
      },
      "id": 112,
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "rate(node_vmstat_oom_kill[5m]) * 60",
          "legendFormat": "OOM kills/min"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "short",
          "custom": {
            "fillOpacity": 25,
            "lineWidth": 2,
            "stacking": {
              "mode": "none"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "row",
      "title": "Reclaim activity (the kernel fighting back)",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 15
      },
      "id": 120,
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "title": "",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 16
      },
      "id": 121,
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "Before the kill, the kernel tries to reclaim memory. These curves spike when it's working hard:\n- **pgmajfault** = page faults serviced from disk (slow path)\n- **pgscan_*** = pages scanned during reclaim\n- **pgsteal_*** = pages successfully evicted\n\nHeavy reclaim + falling MemAvailable = OOM is imminent."
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Major page faults/sec (slow-path memory access)",
      "description": "Every major fault required disk I/O to satisfy. Elevated rate = pages are being evicted faster than they can be brought back.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 20
      },
      "id": 122,
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "rate(node_vmstat_pgmajfault[1m])",
          "legendFormat": "pgmajfault"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops",
          "custom": {
            "fillOpacity": 25,
            "lineWidth": 2,
            "stacking": {
              "mode": "none"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Pages scanned + stolen during reclaim",
      "description": "kswapd is the background reclaimer; direct reclaim happens when allocations stall. The ratio of scan to steal tells you reclaim efficiency.",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 20
      },
      "id": 123,
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "rate(node_vmstat_pgscan_kswapd[1m])",
          "legendFormat": "pgscan_kswapd"
        },
        {
          "refId": "B",
          "expr": "rate(node_vmstat_pgscan_direct[1m])",
          "legendFormat": "pgscan_direct"
        },
        {
          "refId": "C",
          "expr": "rate(node_vmstat_pgsteal_kswapd[1m])",
          "legendFormat": "pgsteal_kswapd"
        },
        {
          "refId": "D",
          "expr": "rate(node_vmstat_pgsteal_direct[1m])",
          "legendFormat": "pgsteal_direct"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "ops",
          "custom": {
            "fillOpacity": 25,
            "lineWidth": 2,
            "stacking": {
              "mode": "none"
            }
          }
        }
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "row",
      "title": "Slab + cache \u2014 kernel-side allocator state",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 28
      },
      "id": 130,
      "collapsed": false,
      "panels": []
    },
    {
      "type": "timeseries",
      "title": "Slab usage (reclaimable vs unreclaimable)",
      "description": "Slab is the kernel's small-object cache. Growing SUnreclaim with no obvious cause is sometimes the precursor to an OOM (kernel memory leak).",
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 29
      },
      "id": 131,
      "targets": [
        {
          "refId": "A",
          "expr": "node_memory_SReclaimable_bytes",
          "legendFormat": "SReclaimable"
        },
        {
          "refId": "B",
          "expr": "node_memory_SUnreclaim_bytes",
          "legendFormat": "SUnreclaim"
        }
      ],
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "unit": "bytes",
